{"reference":"Matthew 2","book":"Matthew","chapter":2,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Reading Matthew’s Scripture Citations","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Matthew’s citation of Micah in the Bethlehem scene belongs to a broader New Testament pattern of reusing or echoing Micah in settings involving Bethlehem, shepherding, household conflict, and Davidic expectation. In the flight to Egypt, Matthew’s use of Hosea 11:1 is a figural rereading: it reads Israel’s exodus pattern as meaningful for this later event without denying that Hosea speaks retrospectively of Israel as God’s child. The Jeremiah quotation after the killing at Bethlehem should likewise not be reduced to a simple direct prediction; later uses of Jeremiah include reinterpretation within Scripture, Jewish textual and liturgical reception, New Testament quotation and allusion, christological reading, and later church theology.","verse_refs":["Matthew 2:5-6","Matthew 2:13-15","Matthew 2:17-18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_matthew_002_74318137109a0088625390f6","reader_path_matthew_002_0387a2ba1dc7c9aa163389fb","reader_path_matthew_002_5a1f63613b996e68c59c2e0f"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_2","text":"Matthew describes eastern magi, a star, gifts, Herod’s alarm, and worship of the child, but does not number the magi or call them kings. The chapter alone narrates Herod’s killing of Bethlehem’s young children. Herodian brutality is independently contextualized more broadly, but this particular event is not independently attested. The opening also places the story under Herodian client rule within a remembered first-century setting shaped by Roman provincial authority, taxation, elite power, and crucifixion.","verse_refs":["Matthew 2:1","Matthew 2:1-12","Matthew 2:15","Matthew 2:16-18","Matthew 2:17-18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_matthew_002_e4226e4f44a881e94e13998a"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}